 — that''s 18. Yes.\n\nTotal 410 emails in inbox. 278 unread.\n\nFormat: headline, then breakdown.","details":[{"type":"text","text":"Final page: 10 items, all unread. No nextPageToken → end.\n\nTotal unread = 18 + 50 + 50 + 50 + 50 + 50 + 10 = 278.\n\nLet me recount carefully:\n- Page 1 (first 50): 0 unread\n- Page 2 (token 361): 0 unread\n- Page 3 (token 311): 18 unread (ids 278 down to 261)\n- Page 4 (token 261): 50 unread (260–211)\n- Page 5 (token 211): 50 unread (210–161)\n- Page 6 (token 161): 50 unread (160–111)\n- Page 7 (token 111): 50 unread (110–61)\n- Page 8 (token 61): 50 unread\n- Page 9 (token 11): 10 unread\n\nTotal = 18+50+50+50+50+50+10 = 278.\n\nTotal inbox emails = 50+50+50+50+50+50+50+50+10 = 410.\n\nSo 278 unread out of 410 in the inbox.\n\nAll 278 unread are from the \"Edge Sender\" test fixture set dated June 2024 (plus a couple of odd ones: \"First\" sender, \"evil\", \"🔒\", \"lf@edge.test\", \"garbage@edge.test\", \"b@edge.test\", \"a@edge.test\", empty senders). These are synthetic edge-case test emails, not real correspondence.\n\nImportant nuance: the 200-message \"Long thread\" is one thread (edge-thread-200) with 200 messages — so 200 of the 278 unread are a single thread. That''s a great insight: the unread count is dominated by one synthetic thread.\n\nLet me verify: the Long thread messages: from 09:05 \"Long thread\" (id 51) through 12:24 (id 250). That''s ids 51–250 = 200 messages. Yes, 200 messages in one thread.\n\nSo: 278 unread, of which 200 are one thread (\"Long thread\", edge-thread-200), and the remaining 78 are other edge-case fixtures.\n\nLet me compute: 278 - 200 = 78.\n\nAlso note: no real unread mail. Every genuine email in the inbox is read. The newest unread is from June 2024.\n\nAlso worth noting: the inbox has 410 messages total, and the unread block is all at the bottom (oldest).\n\nLet me present this well.\n\nAlso note the odd date: one email dated Fri Dec 31 9999 — a fixture.\n\nI should mention the count is exact: 278.\n\nLet me double-check page 3''s count of 18. Page 3 items: 278,277,276,275,274,273,272,271,270,269,268,267,266,265,264,263,262,261 — that''s 18. Yes.\n\nTotal 410 emails in inbox. 278 unread.\n\nFormat: headline, then breakdown."}],"createdAt":1793975294332},{"type":"text","text":"**278 unread — but not one of them is real mail.** Your inbox holds 410 messages; every genuine email in it is read. All 278 unread sit in a single block at the very bottom, dated **June 2024**, from a synthetic test sender.\n\n**Where the 278 come from**\n\n| Source | Unread |\n|---|---|\n| `Re: Long thread` — one thread, 200 messages | 200 |\n| Other edge-case fixtures (MIME, encoding, attachment, date torture tests) | 78 |\n| **Total** | **278** |\n\nThe headline number is almost entirely one artefact: a 200-message thread (`edge-thread-200`) that runs from 09:05 to 12:24 on 10 June 2024, one message per minute. The remaining 78 are the same fixture set — subjects like `mime-nested-100`, `enc-invalid-utf8`, `att-pkpass-zip-bomb`, `max-recipients-4000`, `date-year-9999`, plus a few with deliberately broken headers (empty sender, empty subject, a bare-LF message, one dated 1 Jan 1970).\n\n**What this means practically**\n\n- **Your real unread count is zero.** Nothing from Raj, Priya, Kenji, the bank, or any actual correspondent is sitting unread — the newest genuine email (Kestrel''s newsletter, 26 Sep) is read.\n- **The 278 is a fixture artefact, not a backlog.** Marking them read would clear the badge without losing anything, since they''re test data rather than correspondence.\n- **One caveat on the count itself:** the inbox is paginated and I walked all nine pages to the end, so 278 is exact rather than an estimate. But note the fixture set includes a message dated **31 Dec 9999** and one dated **1 Jan 1970** — if your client sorts by date rather than by arrival, those two will appear at the very top and very bottom of your list respectively, which can make the inbox look stranger than it is.\n\nWant me to mark the whole June 2024 fixture block as read, or leave it as test data?","createdAt":1793975294332}],"metadata":{"modelId":"deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash:floor","provider":"openai-compatible.chat"},"content":"**278 unread — but not one of them is real mail.** Your inbox holds 410 messages; every genuine email in it is read. All 278 unread sit in a single block at the very bottom, dated **June 2024**, from a synthetic test sender.\n\n**Where the 278 come from**\n\n| Source | Unread |\n|---|---|\n| `Re: Long thread` — one thread, 200 messages | 200 |\n| Other edge-case fixtures (MIME, encoding, attachment, date torture tests) | 78 |\n| **Total** | **278** |\n\nThe headline number is almost entirely one artefact: a 200-message thread (`edge-thread-200`) that runs from 09:05 to 12:24 on 10 June 2024, one message per minute. The remaining 78 are the same fixture set — subjects like `mime-nested-100`, `enc-invalid-utf8`, `att-pkpass-zip-bomb`, `max-recipients-4000`, `date-year-9999`, plus a few with deliberately broken headers (empty sender, empty subject, a bare-LF message, one dated 1 Jan 1970).\n\n**What this means practically**\n\n- **Your real unread count is zero.** Nothing from Raj, Priya, Kenji, the bank, or any actual correspondent is sitting unread — the newest genuine email (Kestrel''s newsletter, 26 Sep) is read.\n- **The 278 is a fixture artefact, not a backlog.** Marking them read would clear the badge without losing anything, since they''re test data rather than correspondence.\n- **One caveat on the count itself:** the inbox is paginated and I walked all nine pages to the end, so 278 is exact rather than an estimate. But note the fixture set includes a message dated **31 Dec 9999** and one dated **1 Jan 1970** — if your client sorts by date rather than by arrival, those two will appear at the very top and very bottom of your list respectively, which can make the inbox look stranger than it is.\n\nWant me to mark the wh