Frankfurt → Tokyo Haneda** on 9 Nov: it now departs **18:30** (was 17:55) and lands **14:15** the next day (was 13:40). Seat 14C unchanged. The change came 17 Sep and sits read-but-unactioned in your inbox. Your connection is now **4h10m** in Frankfurt — comfortable, but confirm the ticket was actually reissued, because a schedule change that isn''t accepted can leave you unseated.\n\nFull itinerary on file: **NB 412** Lisbon 10:25 → Frankfurt 14:20, then **NB 908** Frankfurt 18:30 → Haneda 14:15+1, Mon 9 Nov. Paid JPY 214,600.\n\n**2. There is no return flight in this mailbox.** Only the outbound is booked. Your calendar has \"Tokyo trip\" running 9–15 Nov, so you''re due back around the 15th — but nothing confirms it. This is the single biggest gap in the week.\n\n**3. Three calendar conflicts with the trip, none of them cancelled.**\n- **Daily standup, 09:00 UTC** — recurs Mon–Fri straight through 9–13 Nov. On the 9th that''s 09:00–09:15 UTC with a 10:25 departure from Lisbon: you''d be doing it en route to the airport. Tue–Fri it lands at **18:00 JST**, your evenings in Tokyo.\n- **Weekly 1:1 Ana / Raj, Tue 10 Nov 10:00 UTC** = **19:00 JST** — your first evening after landing at 14:15. Move it or decline it.\n- Both live on the **Work** calendar; the standup is a recurring series, so cancel the instances rather than the series.\n\n---\n\n## P2 — The thing I''d actually worry about\n\n**4. Your mailbox has been silent for six weeks, and it isn''t just newsletters.**\nEverything stops at **26 September 2026** — Kestrel''s weekly, Codeyard''s weekly, Paperstack''s monthly, Konkan Bank''s debit alerts, Ledgerline''s monthly invoices. Five unrelated senders going quiet on the same day is not a coincidence; it''s a delivery problem. Given you''re travelling Monday and will be relying on email for boarding passes and booking updates, **check this before you fly** — forwarding rules, filters, or the mailbox connection itself. If real mail is being swallowed, it''s been swallowed for six weeks.\n\n---\n\n## P3 — Quick housekeeping (15 minutes, any time)\n\n**5. A scheduled email is stuck 73 years out.** \"Moodboard for the rebrand\" to yourself is queued for **5 January 2099**. It will never send. Reschedule or delete it.\n\n**6. Decide what to do with the 278 unread test fixtures.** All June 2024, all synthetic, 200 of them one thread. Marking them read clears the badge; deleting them clears the inbox.\n\n**7. Label the bulk mail.** 67 newsletters and 8 promotional blasts are still unlabelled despite the `newsletter` and `promotional` labels existing.\n\n**8. Pick a canonical drive before you write anything.** The two S3 buckets carry an extra `edge/` tree the other five don''t have, and the folder-scoped FTP shows only `Clients/`. If you edit a file on one drive this week, know which copy is authoritative first.\n\n---\n\n## P4 — Client threads that have gone quiet\n\n**9. Aozora Ceramics** — proposal signed 29 Jul, rebrand review was 8 Oct, packaging call 15 Oct. Both past, nothing since. Worth a status note before you''re away.\n\n**10. Kaltwasser GmbH** — Lena Hoffmann''s \"Wartung ab Oktober\" (31 Aug) announced maintenance from October. No follow-up in the mailbox.\n\n**11. Saffron & Sage** — Layla Haddad''s \"Menu print run\" (20 Aug) is the last word in that thread.\n\n---\n\n**One structural note.** Your calendar holds **183 events in the next two weeks**, but roughly 170 of them are `Bulk 1`–`Bulk 294` filler on a calendar literally named \"Bulk\", plus a \"Daily forever\" event on one called \"Edge cases\". That''s the same synthetic pollution as the mail fixtures and the `edge/` drive folder — and it''s why the three real conflicts above were easy to miss. Cleaning the test data out of the calendar is arguably a P2, not a P3.\n\nWant me to start on any of these — draft the return-flight enquiry, cancel the standup instances for 9–13 Nov, or fix that 2099 scheduled email?","createdAt":1793975564809}],"metadata":{"modelI